d bound; at β = 2.2 neither converges, and the crossover theorem is exactly a supremum bound on the single-link influence of the coarse loop at a_c, uniform in the fine configuration, at such a coupling. The datum-dependence read in §7 (the first-scale content differs by a factor 3–8 between the ordered and the cancelling outsides, the second-scale content by 1.5) is an indication of the distance between a typical reading and that supremum, not its measure. THE INSTRUMENT THIS NAMES, in two steps: first, at a fixed datum, the OSCILLATION of the coarse loop's moments over ONE link's value — the sup over U, U′ of the link at the others fixed, a polynomial of bounded degree on SU(2) whose range is computable — which the π rotation of the ladder samples at one point; second, over the datum, which is the theorem. The first is W4's next instrument and is cheap (one link's oscillation is a sweep set per quadrature point of the link's value); the second is the theorem, and the charter's W4 row names its mechanism — the frustrated data weighed by their measure through the chessboard estimate (THE FAR SHORE §5), the typical-data criterion on gauge-invariant coarse variables — not an instrument.
