+# THE STANDING — where the Yang–Mills proof stands, every item at its strength (Edwin Boston III, 2026-11-01 20:3x EST, read from `date`; the trunk seat iii-43)
+
+> Written to the operator's order of 2026-11-01 20:0x: "sift through everything, remove the hedging, acknowledge what we have already accomplished, and return with a thorough, excruciatingly granular assessment of the progress we have truly made." Read for it: THE GROUND (revision 29), THE FAR SHORE, THE COALESCENCE, THE CROSSING, the flux-surface gas (S1), the crossing campaign (§§0–16), the ΑΠΕΡΑΝΤΟΝ road and its Addendum II, the kernel record and the Lean tree on disk, the canon's entries T-768–T-778 and T-794, and the gate runs of record. Every item below carries exactly one status. Nothing is softened and nothing is raised. Where an earlier page stated an item at the wrong strength, §9 names the page, the sentence and the correction.
+
+**The status words.** PROVED: a complete proof on the page, a check by the Lean kernel, or a certificate in directed rounding; where a second seat's signature is recorded, it is named, and where none is recorded, that is said. CONDITIONAL: proved from one named statement that is not proved. IMPLIED: follows from proved items and stated readings by a stated inequality. MEASURED: a deterministic reading of a stated finite or truncated object, in floating point unless marked certified; no Monte Carlo enters any item. CITED: a published theorem of others, used at its published strength. OPEN: not proved; the last attempt and where it stopped are named. Every PROVED item is marked CLASSICAL (with its source) or NEW (this tree's).
+
+## 1. The answer
+
+**The Clay problem for SU(2), reduced.** The Millennium statement asks, for every compact simple gauge group G, for a non-trivial quantum Yang–Mills theory on ℝ⁴ satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader (or Wightman) axioms, with a mass gap Δ > 0. For G = SU(2) this tree has PROVED (THE GROUND, Theorems 7.1, 7.4 and 8.5) that the mass gap and the uniqueness of the vacuum hold for every limit point, given three statements:
+
+- **(E) A CONSTRUCTION.** Wilson lattice measures on a scaling sequence of spacings a_j = a₀2^{−K_j}, with couplings tuned along it, whose Schwinger functions of gauge-invariant observables converge along a subsequence. The axioms of the limit (Euclidean invariance among them) and its non-triviality are further parts of (E) that the Clay statement needs and the gap theorem does not.
+- **(H-I) HYPOTHESIS I** at a fixed physical scale a_c, uniformly in the cutoff: the blocked measure's coarse variables satisfy Dobrushin's condition with an exponential weight, Σ_{y′} C(y, y′)e^{κ|y−y′|} ≤ α < 1 for every coarse point y.
+- **(H-II) HYPOTHESIS II** at the same scale: the field inside the blocks clusters at the block scale, for every coarse configuration.
+
+Then every limit point has a unique vacuum and no spectrum in (0, m), with m = (1/a_c)·min(κ, log 1/ρ′).
+
+**None of (E), (H-I), (H-II) is proved in this tree.** (E) is claimed by others and is not verified here (§8). (H-II) is exact for the one-step perfect decimation and OPEN in the form the reduction consumes, as THE GROUND says in its own text (§6). (H-I) is the crossover, the non-perturbative content of the problem. It is OPEN. Its integrand was measured today in its own currency, THE GROUND's (8.5), on the only exact objects that reach the crossover scale (§7).
+
+**At the strong-coupling end the gap is proved.** The lattice gap at strong coupling for SU(2) on ℤ⁴ is PROVED to β = 0.2452 on proved lemmas alone (Theorem 8.7), and CONDITIONAL to β = 0.4764 on one lemma (Theorem 8.9). Nothing above 0.2452 is proved unconditionally, and nothing above 0.4764 is proved at all.
+
+**What that amounts to.** The continuum mass gap has been turned from a statement about a limit into two inequalities on finite lattice blocks at one fixed physical scale, with every step of the turning proved and its finite skeleton checked by the Lean kernel. The strong-coupling side of the needed inequality is certified. The part that remains open is the part no published method reaches: the blocked measure at effective couplings of order one, between the gas's radius and Bałaban's small-field regime, whose threshold β_UV no paper states.
+
+## 2. PROVED — the frame (THE GROUND, revision 30, 43 pages; OpenTimestamps proofs of its revisions from 13 on, revision 25's in Bitcoin block 973687)
+
+- **Theorem 2.1, the reading of the Alpöge–Sussman corpus.** PROVED as a reading, every quotation with its page in SOURCES.md. NEW. Its content: YMM1 (3D) asserts convergence of the centred plaquette-energy correlations on each torus along the full cutoff sequence, infinite-volume limits along subsequences, the OS axioms (E0)–(E4) and a Wightman field in each clustering component, and a short-distance law that fixes the coupling and proves non-triviality; it asserts no mass gap, and for the original limit neither a unique vacuum nor clustering. The 4D zeroth draft asserts, under inputs it declares clause by clause, limits on ℝ⁴ along subsequences with (E0) in a stated form, translation and hypercubic invariance, reflection positivity, a vacuum and a non-negative Hamiltonian, non-triviality through a two-point law, and O(4) under a displayed hypothesis; it asserts neither clustering, nor a unique vacuum, nor a gap, nor a unique limit, and two of its supporting statements are unwritten. Its authors call the corpus tentative pending verification.
+- **Proposition 3.2**, the standard for a limit obtained by compactness. NEW as stated.
+- **Theorem 7.1.** A gap m > 0 with a unique vacuum is equivalent to the reflection-positive decay S(θf̄·τ_t f) − |S(f)|² ≤ e^{−mt}(S(θf̄·f) − |S(f)|²) for every finite family f and every t ≥ 0. NEW in this form; its halves are CLASSICAL (Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction; the spectral theorem).
+- **Corollary 7.2, Lemma 7.3, Theorem 7.4 (the completion), Corollaries 7.5 and 7.6.** Uniform lattice clustering at a physical rate implies the limit's unique vacuum and gap; the hypothesis holds with r_j = 0 when the transfer matrices satisfy m_lat(j) ≥ m·a_j on the observables' sector. NEW.
+- **Proposition 8.1**, Dobrushin's coefficient of the Doob chain bounds the gap. CLASSICAL (Doob; Dobrushin 1956/1968).
+- **Proposition 8.2, the chain lemma.** NEW.
